#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079683 :tutorial/content "Consider the function $f(x)$ below:\n![](images/e4/e46fcaf79d93513c8b946de51ef451ad8a638936681ce3fc70ffdbe8de023078.svg)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ seems to have a jump discontinuity at $x=-3.$ However, by selecting a particular value for $k$, we can remove the jump discontinuity and make the function continuous.\n\nWe can find this value of $k$ by forcing the left and right-sided limits of the function to be equal at $x=-3\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{x\\to (-3)^-} f(x) = \\lim\\limits_{x\\to (-3)^+} f(x)\n$$\n\nFor the left limit, we have,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-3)^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to (-3)^{-}}(3{x}^{2}+kx) \\\\  & =3(-3)^{2}+k(-3) \\\\  & =27-3k,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand for the right limit, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-3)^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to (-3)^{+}}{x}^{3} \\\\  & =(-3)^{3} \\\\  & =-27.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the left and right limits as equal, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}27-3k & =-27 \\\\ 3k & =54 \\\\ k & =18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, to make $f(x)$ continuous at $x=-3,$ we need to choose $k=18.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "10dabe2c-998a-5731-bcf2-c0ed93bc7f2e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 543 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}]}
